Understand the concept and properties of a Binary Search Tree.
Implement a BST using Python.
Perform insertion, deletion, and searching operations.
Perform inorder, preorder, and postorder traversals.
Find the depth of the tree.
Create a mirror image and a copy of the tree.
Display all leaf nodes.
Display the tree level-wise using a queue.
5. Theory
5.1 Binary Tree
A binary tree is a non-linear data structure in which each node can have at most two children. The two children are called the left child and right child.
5.2 Binary Search Tree (BST)
A Binary Search Tree is a binary tree in which all values in the left subtree are smaller than the root value and all values in the right subtree are greater than the root value. This ordering property makes searching, insertion, and deletion efficient when the tree is reasonably balanced.
Example Patient IDs: 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80
BST structure:
              50
            /    \
          30      70
         /  \    /  \
       20   40  60   80
6. Basic Terminology
Node: A node stores the Patient ID and references to its left and right children.
Root: The topmost node of the tree.
Parent: A node having one or more children.
Child: A node directly connected below another node.
Leaf Node: A node having no left and right children.
Subtree: A tree formed by a node and all its descendants.
Depth: The maximum number of levels from the root to a leaf in this implementation.
7. BST Operations
7.1 Insertion
Adds a new Patient ID. If the ID is smaller than the current node it is inserted in the left subtree; if greater, it is inserted in the right subtree.
7.2 Searching
Compares the required Patient ID with the current node and moves left or right until the ID is found or the search reaches an empty position.
7.3 Deletion
Deletion has three cases: deleting a leaf node, deleting a node with one child, and deleting a node with two children. For two children, the inorder successor can be used.
Case 1: Node is a leaf
A node having no children can be directly deleted
Case 2: Node has one child
The node is replaced by its child.
Case 3: Node has two children
The node is replaced by its inorder successor or inorder predecessor.

7.4 Traversal
The program supports inorder, preorder, and postorder traversal.
7.5 Depth
Depth is calculated recursively as max(left subtree depth, right subtree depth) + 1.
7.6 Mirror
A mirror tree is created by exchanging the left and right subtrees of every node.
7.7 Copy
A separate tree with the same structure and values is created.
The copied tree is independent of the original tree. Changes made to one tree do not affect the other.
7.8 Leaf Nodes
All nodes having no children are displayed.
7.9 Level-wise Traversal
Nodes are displayed level by level using a queue.
